            <ArticleTitle>Formation of antireflection Zn/ZnO core-shell nano-pyramidal arrays by O2+ ion bombardment of Zn surfaces.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>14201-14207</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c7nr03691d</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>ZnO is probably one of the most studied oxides since ZnO nanostructures are a very rich family of nanomaterials with a broad variety of technological applications. Although several chemical techniques offer the possibility to obtain such ZnO nanostructures, here we show that the controlled modification of the zinc surface by low-energy O2+ bombardment leads to the formation of core-shell Zn/ZnO nano-pyramidal arrays that suppress the reflection of light decreasing the reflectivity below 6% in the wavelength range of 300-900 nm. This controlled and scalable protocol opens the door to a broad range of possibilities for the use of ion bombardment to produce surface modifications for technological applications in the field of photoelectric devices and solar cells.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
